       :studio {:title "Six months in Tesa 41"
                :images ["e3f1da5d21"
                         "488ef42b59"
                         "c29e5dffe9"
                         "8998ba35ae"]
                :html "<p>Since 9 May the Armenian Pavilion at Biennale Arte 2026 has been a working sculpture studio. Zadik Zadikian makes his work in Tesa 41, a brick hall with timber trusses in the Arsenale, in front of visitors, Tuesday to Sunday. Nothing in the room is fixed to the walls or the floor.</p>\n<p>The basic element is a plaster unit, cast by hand and pigmented through its full depth, so the colour runs right through the material. Units are stacked, taken down and stacked again. A stack may stand for a day.</p>\n<p>On the floor now is <em>Who&#39;s Afraid of Red, Yellow, and Blue? Remembering Barnett Newman!</em> (2026), a triptych in plaster, acrylic, pure pigment and EPS foam. Beside it are <em>Big Red Bed Where Dreams Come True</em> (2026) and a green unit standing in front of a leaning red slab. In a corner of the hall is <em>Seven Steps to Heaven</em>, a white stair of seven steps that leads nowhere. An Italian flag in green, white and red is being built from units.</p>\n<p>The work has not been continuous. Visa delays kept the team away for much of the summer, and the studio returned to full production in September. Everything that reaches Tesa 41 comes on foot or by boat and is carried in by hand.</p>\n<p>Tony Shafrazi, the pavilion&#39;s curator, describes it as &quot;seeing and studying art in the making.&quot; The studio is open until Sunday 22 November.</p>\n"}
       :serra {:title "Zadikians: Richard Serra's studio, 1973–74"
               :images ["8b51916405"
                        "430e8e9073"
                        "795ed73e3d"]
               :html "<p>Zadik Zadikian came to New York in 1973 and began working as an assistant to Richard Serra. Serra was then taking drawing off the sheet and onto the wall: unstretched Belgian linen covered in black paintstick, stapled up and cut to fit the room. Zadikian worked on those drawings.</p>\n<p>In 1974 Serra titled one of his first installation drawings <em>Zadikians</em>. It is now in the Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum, New York (Panza Collection). He titled another <em>Shafrazi</em>. Both were shown at Leo Castelli&#39;s gallery from 12 to 26 October 1974, in Serra&#39;s first exhibition of drawings. In 2011 Serra described <em>Abstract Slavery</em> (1974) as &quot;the result of the collective effort of Zadik Zadikian, Tony Shafrazi, and myself.&quot; The title, Zadikian recalls, came from Shafrazi&#39;s description of the work.</p>\n<p>Zadikian also recalls suggesting the idea behind the drawings to Serra: &quot;Why don&#39;t you make your paper become your figure, and the wall become the ground?&quot; That is his account. The record shows his name on a Serra title in a museum collection.</p>\n<p>It was in Serra&#39;s studio that Zadikian met Tony Shafrazi, in 1973, after Serra and Shafrazi came back from Texas, where they had helped Nancy Holt complete Robert Smithson&#39;s <em>Amarillo Ramp</em>. Zadikian remembers Serra introducing Shafrazi as &quot;Armenian as well.&quot; Shafrazi put it in print in 1984: &quot;Richard Serra introduced me to Zadik Zadikian in 1973.&quot;</p>\n<p>Serra died in March 2024. Fifty-three years after that meeting, Shafrazi is the curator of Zadikian&#39;s pavilion in Venice.</p>\n"}
       :tehran {:title "Gold Bricks, Tehran, 1978"
                :images ["dbac198dd8"
                         "8b51916405"
                         "52a0b99f06"]
                :html "<p>In the summer of 1978 Zadik Zadikian spent three months in Tehran, where Tony Shafrazi was preparing to open a gallery. At a brickworks outside the city he watched workers stack raw clay bricks to dry, with gaps left between them so the air could pass. &quot;This is a fantastic way of stacking,&quot; he recalls thinking.</p>\n<p>He had about a thousand bricks made by a local brick company, sanded them and covered them in gold leaf. <em>Gold Bricks</em> opened on 31 October 1978 at the Tony Shafrazi Gallery, Tehran. The city had been under martial law since September. It was the gallery&#39;s first exhibition and its only one: the Shah left Iran a few months later and the gallery did not reopen. After the Revolution the bricks were stolen from storage. &quot;We lost each and every brick in the revolution,&quot; Zadikian told <em>ARTnews</em> in 2025.</p>\n<p>What he kept from Tehran was the gap. His units have been stacked with space between them ever since. &quot;I leave spaces between my blocks, my units, so that they can breathe,&quot; he says.</p>\n<p>A year after Tehran, on 16 October 1979, Zadikian opened Shafrazi&#39;s New York gallery. <em>Zadik Zadikian: Sculptures</em> was the first exhibition of the Tony Shafrazi Gallery in New York, and it showed stacks of bricks he had cast by hand. The same gallery later gave Keith Haring his first major solo exhibition (1982) and, in <em>Champions</em> (1983), showed Zadikian alongside Jean-Michel Basquiat, Haring and Kenny Scharf.</p>\n"}
       :downtown {:title "Rooms in yellow and gold: New York, 1973–78"
                  :images ["8c28815040"
                           "3800768b0a"
                           "f4c0ffbcd5"
                           "3b9aa96133"]
                  :html "<p>Before Zadik Zadikian made units, he made rooms.</p>\n<p>At 112 Greene Street, the artist-run space in SoHo, he painted the whole gallery yellow, fixtures and all, and opened it to the street (1973–74). He calls the piece <em>Yellow Space</em>. &quot;I removed the windows off the frames… so the place would breathe,&quot; he recalls. On 9 November 1974 he made <em>Billboard Painting</em> at 111 Varick Street for Alanna Heiss&#39;s Institute for Art and Urban Resources.</p>\n<p>In the mid-1970s he covered his own apartment and studio in industrial gold foil. Walls, ceilings, pipes, the radiator and the water heater all went gold. He received a CAPS grant for the gilded studio in 1975, and the gold rooms at 9 Jay Street date from 1975–76. &quot;It was covered, unified, very democratic,&quot; he says.</p>\n<p>Next came P.S.1, the former public school in Long Island City that opened as an art space in 1976. Zadikian gilded its entrance, and by his account it stayed gold for about five years. The gold was in place by April 1978, and P.S.1&#39;s records list him in its Special Projects of winter 1978.</p>\n<p>The bricks of P.S.1 led him to the unit. &quot;This is built with bricks, why don&#39;t I just start gilding bricks… That&#39;s how I got into units,&quot; he says.</p>\n<p>None of these rooms survives. They were made for one place and later painted over or taken down, like much of the work made downtown in those years. Photographs are what remains.</p>\n"}
       :accademia {:title "Seven assistants from the Accademia"
                   :images ["58b122556e"
                            "4061cedbde"
                            "81f39add05"
                            "eff02ea41a"]
                   :html "<p>In the 1970s Zadik Zadikian was Richard Serra&#39;s assistant. In Venice he has seven assistants of his own. All seven are students at the Accademia di Belle Arti di Venezia, working in the Armenian Pavilion under a convention with the Accademia.</p>\n<p>They do the studio&#39;s daily work in public, Tuesday to Sunday. They mix and pour plaster, pigment the units and sand them. They cut EPS foam forms, print parts in resin and build in timber. Every piece then has to be carried into Tesa 41 by hand. In October the team brought <em>Seven Steps to Heaven</em> along the quay in sections, then skimmed, sanded and painted it white in the studio.</p>\n<p>The team also talks about what it is making, and the studio films those conversations. Standing in front of the stair, one of the team said: &quot;I think that the ultimate point of a sculpture like this is that, being devoid of points of reference, you fill it with yourself.&quot; Another called the work a <em>viaggio</em>, a trip: &quot;There is a start, there is a finish, but the finish is not so clear.&quot;</p>\n<p>Zadikian knows the job from the other side. In 1974 Serra titled one of his installation drawings <em>Zadikians</em>, after the assistant who had helped make it. Fifty years later the question of who makes the work is part of the daily life of the pavilion.</p>\n<p>Interviews with students, in Italian or English, can be arranged before the pavilion closes on Sunday 22 November.</p>\n"}
       :armenia {:title "Yerevan to Venice: RETURN and the national participation"
                 :images ["f35e171351"
                          "06087305db"
                          "e42e19d42e"]
                 :html "<p>Zadik Zadikian left Soviet Armenia in 1965. Sixty years later, <em>RETURN</em> (5 May – 1 December 2025) at the Cafesjian Center for the Arts in Yerevan showed his <em>Foreigners</em> series (2015–17), gilded clay figures. The Center describes it as his first exhibition in Armenia.</p>\n<p>In October 2025 a national jury chose his project, from seven submissions, to represent the Republic of Armenia at the 61st International Art Exhibition – La Biennale di Venezia. It was first announced as <em>Ode to Lord Byron</em>, after the poet who studied Armenian with the monks of San Lazzaro degli Armeni, the island monastery in the Venetian lagoon. By March 2026 the title was <em>The Studio</em>. The commissioner is Svetlana Sahakyan of the Ministry of Education, Science, Culture and Sport. The curators are Tony Shafrazi and Tina Chakarian.</p>\n<p>Armenia has taken part in La Biennale as a nation since 1995. In 2015 its pavilion, <em>Armenity</em>, on San Lazzaro, received the Golden Lion for best national participation.</p>\n<p>Armenia has run through Zadikian&#39;s work for decades. In the 1980s he made gilded heads and reliefs after Armenian kings, including <em>Tigran the Great</em> (1980). &quot;Maybe because I was missing Armenia a great deal… or I was looking for identity,&quot; he says of those years.</p>\n<p>In Venice the units carry no national symbols. A proposed work, <em>Armenian Flag Stack</em> (2026), would stack pigmented plaster units in the colours of the Armenian flag. One rendering places it in Republic Square, Yerevan.</p>\n"}
       :people {:title "People"
                :people [{:name "Zadik Zadikian"
                          :role "Artist"
                          :html "<p>Zadik Zadikian is a sculptor. He was born in Yerevan, Soviet Armenia, and lives and works in Los Angeles. He left Armenia in 1965 and reached the United States by way of Beirut. In San Francisco he worked as an assistant to the sculptor Beniamino Bufano. In New York from 1973 he was Richard Serra&#39;s assistant, and Serra titled the installation drawing <em>Zadikians</em> (1974, Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum) after him. He opened both of Tony Shafrazi&#39;s galleries, in Tehran (1978) and New York (1979). Recent work includes <em>Path to Nine</em> (2024), shown in <em>Solid Gold</em> at the Brooklyn Museum, and <em>RETURN</em> (2025) at the Cafesjian Center for the Arts, Yerevan.</p>\n"
                          :text "Zadik Zadikian is a sculptor. He was born in Yerevan, Soviet Armenia, and lives and works in Los Angeles. He left Armenia in 1965 and reached the United States by way of Beirut. In San Francisco he worked as an assistant to the sculptor Beniamino Bufano. In New York from 1973 he was Richard Serra's assistant, and Serra titled the installation drawing Zadikians (1974, Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum) after him. He opened both of Tony Shafrazi's galleries, in Tehran (1978) and New York (1979). Recent work includes Path to Nine (2024), shown in Solid Gold at the Brooklyn Museum, and RETURN (2025) at the Cafesjian Center for the Arts, Yerevan."
                          :short "Zadik Zadikian, born in Yerevan, is a Los Angeles-based sculptor who works with cast, pigmented and gilded units. He assisted Richard Serra in 1970s New York and opened both of Tony Shafrazi's galleries, in Tehran (1978) and New York (1979)."}
                         {:name "Tony Shafrazi"
                          :role "Curator"
                          :html "<p>Tony Shafrazi (b. 1943, Abadan, Iran) is an art dealer and curator. Born to Armenian parents, he studied at the Royal College of Art in London and settled in New York at the end of the 1960s. In 1973 he helped Nancy Holt and Richard Serra complete Robert Smithson&#39;s <em>Amarillo Ramp</em>, and that year Serra introduced him to Zadik Zadikian. He opened the Tony Shafrazi Gallery in Tehran in 1978 and in New York in 1979, both times with Zadikian. The New York gallery gave Keith Haring his first major solo exhibition (1982) and showed the paintings Andy Warhol and Jean-Michel Basquiat made together (1985). He now works as Gallery Without Walls.</p>\n"
                          :text "Tony Shafrazi (b. 1943, Abadan, Iran) is an art dealer and curator. Born to Armenian parents, he studied at the Royal College of Art in London and settled in New York at the end of the 1960s. In 1973 he helped Nancy Holt and Richard Serra complete Robert Smithson's Amarillo Ramp, and that year Serra introduced him to Zadik Zadikian. He opened the Tony Shafrazi Gallery in Tehran in 1978 and in New York in 1979, both times with Zadikian. The New York gallery gave Keith Haring his first major solo exhibition (1982) and showed the paintings Andy Warhol and Jean-Michel Basquiat made together (1985). He now works as Gallery Without Walls."
                          :short "Tony Shafrazi is an art dealer and curator, born in Abadan, Iran, in 1943. His New York gallery opened in 1979 with Zadik Zadikian and gave Keith Haring his first major solo exhibition in 1982."}
                         {:name "Tina Chakarian"
                          :role "Curator"
                          :html "<p>Tina Chakarian is a curator based in Boston and Yerevan. Born in Beirut, she studied visual arts at UCLA and Tufts University. She has worked to advance Armenia&#39;s presence at La Biennale di Venezia since 2015, when she was part of the advisory team for <em>Armenity</em>, the Armenian Pavilion on the island of San Lazzaro degli Armeni that received the Golden Lion for best national participation. Since 2018 she has been Development Director of the Armenian Pavilion, raising funds and working on its exhibitions and events. She is a curator of <em>The Studio</em> with Tony Shafrazi.</p>\n"
                          :text "Tina Chakarian is a curator based in Boston and Yerevan. Born in Beirut, she studied visual arts at UCLA and Tufts University. She has worked to advance Armenia's presence at La Biennale di Venezia since 2015, when she was part of the advisory team for Armenity, the Armenian Pavilion on the island of San Lazzaro degli Armeni that received the Golden Lion for best national participation. Since 2018 she has been Development Director of the Armenian Pavilion, raising funds and working on its exhibitions and events. She is a curator of The Studio with Tony Shafrazi."
                          :short "Tina Chakarian is a curator based in Boston and Yerevan. She has worked to advance Armenia's presence at La Biennale di Venezia since 2015 and is a curator of The Studio."}
                         {:name "Aram Zadikian"
                          :role "Director, Zadik Zadikian Studio"
                          :html "<p>Aram Zadikian is the director of Zadik Zadikian Studio and the artist&#39;s son. In Venice he runs the studio in Tesa 41: production, the team of seven assistants from the Accademia di Belle Arti di Venezia, deliveries into the Arsenale, and the pavilion&#39;s press and website. He works beside his father on each piece, from the first drawing through the 3D model to the full-scale form, and he made most of the renderings and photographs in the pavilion&#39;s press library. Requests for studio visits and interviews go to him.</p>\n"
                          :text "Aram Zadikian is the director of Zadik Zadikian Studio and the artist's son. In Venice he runs the studio in Tesa 41: production, the team of seven assistants from the Accademia di Belle Arti di Venezia, deliveries into the Arsenale, and the pavilion's press and website. He works beside his father on each piece, from the first drawing through the 3D model to the full-scale form, and he made most of the renderings and photographs in the pavilion's press library. Requests for studio visits and interviews go to him."
                          :short "Aram Zadikian is the director of Zadik Zadikian Studio and the artist's son. He runs The Studio in Tesa 41 and works beside his father on each piece."}]}
       :chronology {:title "Chronology"
                    :items [{:when "Early years"
                             :html "Born in Yerevan, Soviet Armenia, where he studies sculpture."}
                            {:when "1965"
                             :html "Leaves Soviet Armenia. By his account he spends months in Istanbul and then Beirut, where he makes plaster portrait busts."}
                            {:when "1968"
                             :html "Living in the San Francisco Bay Area. Works as an assistant to the sculptor Beniamino Bufano."}
                            {:when "1970"
                             :html "By his account, a solo exhibition at the gallery La Cassapanca in Rome."}
                            {:when "1973"
                             :html "Moves to New York and becomes an assistant to Richard Serra. Meets Tony Shafrazi in Serra&#39;s studio."}
                            {:when "1973–74"
                             :html "Paints the whole of 112 Greene Street yellow, fixtures included. He calls the piece <em>Yellow Space</em>."}
                            {:when "1974"
                             :html "Serra titles two installation drawings <em>Zadikians</em> (Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum) and <em>Shafrazi</em>, shown at Leo Castelli, 12–26 October. Serra later calls <em>Abstract Slavery</em> (1974) &quot;the collective effort of Zadik Zadikian, Tony Shafrazi, and myself&quot;."}
                            {:when "1974"
                             :html "<em>Billboard Painting</em>, 111 Varick Street, New York, 9 November, organised by Alanna Heiss&#39;s Institute for Art and Urban Resources."}
                            {:when "1975"
                             :html "CAPS grant for a gilded studio. Covers his apartment and studio in industrial gold foil (9 Jay Street, 1975–76)."}
                            {:when "1978"
                             :html "Listed in P.S.1&#39;s Special Projects (15 January – 18 February). His gilded entrance at P.S.1 is in place by April."}
                            {:when "1978"
                             :html "<em>Gold Bricks</em>, about a thousand gilded bricks, opens on 31 October at the Tony Shafrazi Gallery, Tehran, the gallery&#39;s first and only exhibition. The bricks are later stolen from storage."}
                            {:when "1979"
                             :html "<em>Zadik Zadikian: Sculptures</em> (16 October – 5 November), the first exhibition of the Tony Shafrazi Gallery, New York."}
                            {:when "1980"
                             :html "Solo exhibition, Tony Shafrazi Gallery (23 March – 19 April), including <em>Tigran the Great</em>, gilded."}
                            {:when "1983"
                             :html "<em>Champions</em>, Tony Shafrazi Gallery (15 January – 19 February), with Jean-Michel Basquiat, Keith Haring and Kenny Scharf."}
                            {:when "1985"
                             :html "<em>New California Artist VIII: Zadik Zadikian. Sculpture</em>, Newport Harbor Art Museum, with a catalogue by Paul Schimmel."}
                            {:when "1986"
                             :html "<em>Sculpture Myths</em>, Tony Shafrazi Gallery (13 December 1986 – 19 January 1987)."}
                            {:when "2002"
                             :html "<em>Caravan</em>: six gilded canvases after Armenian manuscript miniatures, shown on flatbed trucks around Los Angeles."}
                            {:when "2015–17"
                             :html "The <em>Foreigners</em> series of gilded clay figures."}
                            {:when "2024"
                             :html "<em>Path to Nine</em>: 999 gilded plaster ingots in eighteen stacks, in <em>Solid Gold</em>, Brooklyn Museum (16 November 2024 – 6 July 2025)."}
                            {:when "2025"
                             :html "<em>RETURN</em>, Cafesjian Center for the Arts, Yerevan (5 May – 1 December), described by the Center as his first exhibition in Armenia."}
                            {:when "2025"
                             :html "<em>Made in USA</em>, a column of gilded plaster units, with Tony Shafrazi / Gallery Without Walls at Independent 20th Century, New York (September)."}
                            {:when "2025"
                             :html "In October a national jury selects his project for the Republic of Armenia at La Biennale di Venezia."}
                            {:when "2026"
                             :html "<em>The Studio</em>, Pavilion of the Republic of Armenia, 61st International Art Exhibition – La Biennale di Venezia. Tesa 41, Arsenale Militare, 9 May – 22 November. Curators Tony Shafrazi and Tina Chakarian; commissioner Svetlana Sahakyan."}]}
       :quotes {:title "Quotes"
                :quotes [{:speaker "Zadik Zadikian"
                          :text "You tune into simplicity, blocks of colour."
                          :source "armenianpavilion.org, home page (checked 31 October 2026)"}
                         {:speaker "Tony Shafrazi"
                          :text "Seeing and studying art in the making."
                          :source "labiennale.org, page for <em>The Studio</em>, and armenianpavilion.org (checked 31 October 2026)"}
                         {:speaker "Zadik Zadikian"
                          :text "I leave spaces between my blocks, my units, so that they can breathe."
                          :source "interview with the pavilion team, Venice, September 2026"}
                         {:speaker "Zadik Zadikian"
                          :text "Each unit you can see all around, instead of like a wall."
                          :source "interview with the pavilion team, Venice, September 2026"}
                         {:speaker "Tony Shafrazi"
                          :text "It's haptic. That's what makes it."
                          :source "Armenian Pavilion press release, December 2025"}
                         {:speaker "Zadik Zadikian"
                          :text "For me, gold is not currency. It is frequency."
                          :source "Zadik Zadikian, Artist Statement, <em>The Studio</em>, 2026"}]}
